🚨 **BLOCKING: Misspelled Russian autonym — «Руский» must be «Руский»**

The Settings page shows the Russian language name with a single «с»; the correct Russian autonym is «Руский» (double «с»). The tests lock the typo in, so the suite stays green while shipping a misspelled language name.

`lib/pages/settings_page.dart:22`:

```dart
final languageName = switch (locale) {
  const Locale('en') => 'English',
  const Locale('ru') => 'Руский', // ← misspelled; correct: «Руский»
  const Locale('es') => 'Español',
  _ => l10n.systemLanguage,
};
```

Fix all three places the misspelling lives:

1. `lib/pages/settings_page.dart:22` — `'Руский'` → `'Руский'`
2. `test/settings_page_test.dart:51` — `expect(find.text('Руский'), findsOneWidget);` → `'Руский'`
3. The PR description repeats «Руский» as well.

For a localization PR, the language picker misspelling the name of a supported language is a user-facing content bug. Everything else in this change verified cleanly locally (`flutter analyze` clean, `flutter test` → 42 passed, `flutter gen-l10n` output in sync with the ARBs), so this is the only must-fix.

💡 **SUGGESTION: Autonyms hardcode the supported-locale list a third time**

The supported locales now live in three places: `appSupportedLocales` (blocks), `AppLocalizations.supportedLocales` (generated), and this switch. If a locale is ever added (say `de`) without touching this switch, Settings will show "System default" as the current language while the app renders in the new language — a silent wrong-state display.

Consider one const map colocated with the cubit so adding a locale is a single-place change:

```dart
// packages/blocks/lib/src/settings/locale_cubit.dart
/// Autonyms of [appSupportedLocales]; shown unchanged in every language.
const appLanguageNames = <Locale, String>{
  Locale('en'): 'English',
  Locale('ru'): 'Руский',
  Locale('es'): 'Español',
};

// lib/pages/settings_page.dart
final languageName = appLanguageNames[locale] ?? l10n.systemLanguage;
```

Optional extra consistency: `MaterialApp.supportedLocales` could reuse the generated `AppLocalizations.supportedLocales` instead of the blocks copy — today the two lists agree on membership but differ in order (generated: en, es, ru; blocks: en, ru, es), which is a subtle trap for future readers.

